            <ArticleTitle>High headache prevalence among women with hemochromatosis: the Nord-Trøndelag health study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>786-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In this large cross-sectional population-based study, 51,272 persons responded to a headache questionnaire and were screened for hemochromatosis. Phenotypic hemochromatosis and the C282Y/C282Y genotype were both associated with an 80% increase in headache prevalence evident only among women. The reason for this association is unclear, but one may speculate that iron overload alters the threshold for triggering a headache by disturbing neuronal function.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
